{0 Error handling}

Parseff exposes four main APIs for errors:

- {!val:Parseff.fail}: fail with a string message
- {!val:Parseff.error}: fail with a typed {!type:Parseff.custom_error} value
- {!val:Parseff.expect}: relabel [Expected] failures at a parser's start
- {!val:Parseff.context} and {!val:Parseff.cut}: name constructs and seal
  backtracking

{1 Built-in and custom errors}

{b Built-in errors} come from Parseff itself: primitive mismatches
([Expected]), messages from {!val:Parseff.fail} ([Failed]), or limit checks
([Limit_exceeded], [Depth_limit_exceeded], [Backtrack_window_exceeded]).
{!val:Parseff.render_error} formats all of them out of the box.

{b Custom errors} are constructors you add to the extensible variant
{!type:Parseff.custom_error}, raised with {!val:Parseff.error} and returned in
[kind] as [Custom]. Use them when callers need to match on domain-specific
validation failures in code. Pass [~custom] to {!val:Parseff.render_error} to
format them.

{@ocaml[
type Parseff.custom_error += Out_of_range of int

let rating () =
  let n = Char.code (Parseff.digit ()) - Char.code '0' in
  if n < 1 || n > 5 then Parseff.error (Out_of_range n) else n

let () =
  match Parseff.parse "7" rating with
  | Ok n -> Printf.printf "Got %d\n" n
  | Error { kind = Custom (Out_of_range n); _ } ->
      Printf.printf "%d is out of range\n" n
  | Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error e)
]}

{1 [fail] and [error]}

Both {!val:Parseff.fail} and {!val:Parseff.error} fail at the current position
and participate in normal backtracking: {!val:Parseff.or_}, {!val:Parseff.many},
{!val:Parseff.optional}, and other combinators absorb them and try the next
alternative. Call {!val:Parseff.cut} first to make a failure fatal.

{@ocaml skip[
val fail :
  ?from:int -> ?upto:int -> ?notes:note list -> ?help:string -> string -> 'a

val error :
  ?from:int -> ?upto:int -> ?notes:note list -> ?help:string -> custom_error -> 'a
]}

- [~from] and [~upto] (both default to the current position) set the underlined
  byte range.
- [~notes] attaches annotations: a note with [at = Some range] underlines that
  range in the excerpt; [at = None] prints a [* note:] line.
- [~help] prints a [* help:] line after any notes.

{@ocaml[
let octet () =
  let start = Parseff.position () in
  let n =
    int_of_string
      (Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 ~label:"digit" (fun c ->
           c >= '0' && c <= '9'))
  in
  if n <= 255 then n
  else (
    Parseff.cut ();
    Parseff.fail ~from:start
      ~notes:[ { Parseff.at = None; text = "octets go from 0 to 255" } ]
      "octet out of range")

let () =
  match
    Parseff.parse "10.300" (fun () ->
        let a = octet () in
        let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
        (a, octet ()))
  with
  | Error e -> print_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"address" e)
  | Ok _ -> ()
]}

Output:

{v
address:1:4: [error] octet out of range
1 | 10.300
  |    ^^^
  * note: octets go from 0 to 255
v}

{1 [expect]}

{!val:Parseff.expect} replaces [Expected] failures at the parser's starting
position with [expected <description>]. Failures past the starting position, as
well as [fail] and [error], are preserved.

{@ocaml skip[val expect : string -> (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let dot () = Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.')
]}

{1 [context] and [cut]}

{!val:Parseff.context} names a grammar construct so failures inside it report
[* while parsing <name>]. {!val:Parseff.cut} seals backtracking frames inside
the enclosing [context] so a later failure becomes the parse error instead of
retrying outer alternatives. See {!page:combinators}.

To collect multiple errors and keep parsing, see the
{{!page-"error-recovery"}Error recovery} guide.

{1 Furthest-failure selection}

When a parse fails, Parseff reports the failure at the highest byte offset
reached across all branches, including branches abandoned by [or_], [many],
[sep_by], [optional], or [fold_*]:

- Multiple primitive mismatches at that position merge into one sorted,
  deduplicated [Expected] list: [expected "else", "if", or "while", found 'x'].
- At the same position, [fail] and [error] win over [Expected]; between two
  [fail]/[error] calls, the first wins.
- [literal] fails at the first differing byte and expects the remaining suffix.
- End-of-input failures preserve what was expected: ["[1, 2"] reports
  [expected ',' or ']', found end of input].
- {!val:Parseff.commit} discards failures at or beyond its position so a
  committed branch reports only its own expectations.

{1 The [error] value}

{@ocaml skip[
type error = private {
  severity : severity;
  kind : error_kind;
  range : range;
  location : location option;
  contexts : context list;
  notes : note list;
  help : string option;
  excerpt : excerpt;
}
]}

[error] and its nested types are [private] (readable and matchable, not
constructible), except {!type:Parseff.range} and {!type:Parseff.note}.

[kind] cases:

- [Expected { items; found; more }]: primitive mismatch. [items] holds up to 64
  sorted, unique expectations ([Char], [Literal], [Label], [End_of_input]);
  [more] counts overflow items. [found] is [Text] (input snippet), [End] (EOF),
  [Short n] (short fixed-width read), or [Value] (mismatched binary integer).
- [Failed msg]: raised by {!val:Parseff.fail}, or ["no alternative matched"]
  for [one_of []].
- [Custom e]: raised by {!val:Parseff.error}. Match with a fallback [_] case.
- [Limit_exceeded { limit }]: exceeded [~at_most] on [take_while], [take_until],
  or [many].
- [Depth_limit_exceeded { limit }]: exceeded [~max_depth] on {!val:Parseff.rec_}
  or stack depth in {!val:Parseff.fold_right}.
- [Backtrack_window_exceeded { limit; oldest }]: exceeded [~backtrack_window] in
  a streaming parse.

{1 Rendering errors}

{!val:Parseff.render_error} formats an error with its source excerpt, carets,
notes, help, and open constructs; {!val:Parseff.error_message} returns the
one-line message alone.

{v
input.json:4:5: [error] expected ',' or '}', found '"'
2 |     "config": {
  | ╭─────────────╯
3 | │     "a": 1
4 | │     "b": 2
  | ╰─────^
  * while parsing object, from 2:13
  * while parsing object, from 1:1
v}

Formatting details:

- {b Header}: [name:line:col: [error] message] (or [line:col] without [~name];
  [byte N] with no excerpt when the line is unknown in streaming). Lists up to
  8 expected items before [, or N more alternatives].
- {b Columns and carets}: header and context columns count Unicode code points,
  while [location.byte_col] counts bytes. Carets and note underlines count
  terminal cells (wide characters like [名] take two, combining marks take
  zero), preserve tabs, and cover escaped control/invalid UTF-8 bytes
  ([\x1B], [\u{00A0}]).
- {b Streaming dropped prefixes}: when streaming dropped the start of the error
  line and [~source] is omitted, the header still reports the code-point column
  unless the error starts inside the dropped prefix (in which case it falls back
  to [location.byte_col], and contexts in that prefix show [from byte N]). The
  excerpt prefixes [...] and places carets only under retained bytes. Pass
  [~source] if you have the full input.
- {b Sources}: line and column restart at each {!val:Parseff.Source.parse}, so
  for its errors pass as [~source] the input from
  {!val:Parseff.Source.offset}, read before the parse, onward.
- {b Contexts}: prints up to 5 innermost [* while parsing] lines (plus one more
  rather than hiding a single line). Delimited constructs spanning lines render
  the [╭─╯] / [│] / [╰──^] bar, collapsing multi-line gaps to [...].
- {b Notes and help}: ranged notes underline with [-]; unranged notes print as
  [* note:]; [~help] (and built-in hints for depth/window limits) prints as
  [* help:].
- {b Custom errors}: pass [~custom]; without it, [Custom] prints
  [custom error (no ~custom printer given)].

{1 Migrating from 0.4}

[Error { pos; error }] with polymorphic variants was replaced by the [error]
record:

{@ocaml skip[
(* before *)
| Error { pos; error = `Expected msg } -> Printf.printf "%d: %s\n" pos msg
| Error { error = `Too_large n; _ } -> ...
| Error { error = `Failure msg; _ } -> ...
| Error { error = `Unexpected_end_of_input; _ } -> ...

(* after *)
| Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"input" e)
| Error { kind = Custom (Too_large n); _ } -> ...
| Error { kind = Failed msg; _ } -> ...
| Error { kind = Expected { found = End; _ }; _ } -> ...
]}

Declare custom errors via [type Parseff.custom_error += Too_large of int].
Invalid UTF-8 and mismatched binary integers ([BE.int16], etc.) are now
[Expected] items ([valid UTF-8], [int16be 0xFFFE]), EOF errors preserve
expectations, and the failure offset is [e.range.start].
